+  {
+    slug:      '2bhk-apartment-layout-planning-guide',
+    title:     '2 BHK Apartment Layout: A Space Planning Guide Before You Finalise Anything',
+    metaTitle: '2 BHK Apartment Layout Planning Guide — indeva studio',
+    metaDesc:  'How to plan a 2 BHK apartment layout that actually works — zoning, circulation, storage and furniture placement for real Indian households.',
+    category:  'spatial planning',
+    readTime:  '9 min read',
+    date:      'August 2026',
+    city:      'Delhi NCR',
+    heroImage: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1600210492486-724fe5c67fb0?w=1200&h=675&fit=crop&q=80',
+    heroAlt:   '2 BHK apartment living and dining layout — indeva studio',
+    heroCredit:{ name: 'R ARCHITECTURE', url: 'https://unsplash.com/@rarchitecture_melbourne' },
+    intro: 'Most people planning a 2 BHK start with colour palettes and furniture catalogues. That is backwards. A 2 BHK layout is fundamentally a space planning problem: how people move through the flat, where they sit, where they work, and where things get stored. In a 2 BHK there is no slack. A poorly placed sofa in a 4 BHK is an inconvenience; in a 2 BHK it can block the only route to the kitchen.',
+    sections: [
+      { type: 'h2', text: 'Start By Assessing the Existing Floor Plan Honestly' },
+      { type: 'p', text: 'Before changing anything, confirm three things: which walls are structural versus non-structural (do not assume — check with your builder\'s plan or a structural engineer), where the fixed points are (doors, windows, plumbing stacks), and what your primary circulation route already is. In most 2 BHK apartments this route is main door to living/dining to kitchen, with a second route to the bedrooms. Everything else is negotiable; this route usually is not.' },
+      { type: 'h2', text: 'Common Layout Mistakes in 2 BHK Apartments' },
+      { type: 'list', items: ['Treating living and dining as one undifferentiated space with no zoning device', 'Buying furniture before measuring circulation space', 'Letting furniture or storage block the transition into the kitchen', 'Planning the second bedroom as single-purpose when it will actually serve multiple functions', 'Over-committing to open shelving that will not be maintained in daily use'] },
+      { type: 'h2', text: 'Living and Dining Zoning' },
+      { type: 'p', text: 'Orient the dining table closer to the kitchen entrance to shorten the serving distance. Anchor the living area against the longest unbroken wall, ideally opposite or adjacent to the main window. Use a rug under the seating group, not the dining table, to visually separate the two zones without a physical divider. In wider halls (roughly 13 feet or more), a low console or partial screen can mark the transition without blocking light.' },
+      { type: 'h2', text: 'Furniture Circulation: How Much Clearance to Leave' },
+      { type: 'p', text: 'There is no single mandated number, but these are practical working ranges: main walking route 36 to 48 inches, space between sofa and centre table 12 to 18 inches, clearance around the dining table 30 to 42 inches from table edge to wall, and 24 to 36 inches between bed and wardrobe. Treat the lower end as genuinely minimum, not comfortable, and always confirm against your actual furniture dimensions rather than a generic assumption.' },
+      { type: 'h2', text: 'Kitchen and Bedroom Planning' },
+      { type: 'p', text: 'Most 2 BHK kitchens are a single-wall galley, a parallel galley, or an L-shape, usually fixed by the civil layout. Keep sink, stove and refrigerator in a logical working sequence, and decide early whether the kitchen needs to house a washing machine, since that affects plumbing and electrical planning. In the master bedroom, avoid placing the bed directly under a window behind the headboard, and keep wardrobe placement clear of the door swing. The second bedroom should be planned for flexibility, not locked into one function.' },
+      { type: 'h2', text: 'Storage, Work-From-Home and Guest Requirements' },
+      { type: 'p', text: 'Storage should be planned as a system across the whole flat: wardrobes sized to actual clothing volume, a dedicated shoe and entry unit near the main door, and loft storage for infrequently used items. If work-from-home is a permanent requirement, it needs a genuine, electrically planned spot, not a laptop on the dining table. For occasional guests, a sofa-cum-bed or daybed in the second bedroom is usually more realistic than permanently sacrificing square footage to a guest room that sits empty most of the year.' },
+      { type: 'h2', text: 'When Walls Should and Should Not Be Changed' },
+      { type: 'p', text: 'Removing a non-structural partition purely blocking light or flow is often worth it once confirmed as non-structural. Removing bedroom or bathroom walls for a marginally larger living room is usually not — the loss of a genuinely closable room, or the cost of relocating plumbing stacks, tends to outweigh the aesthetic gain.' },
+      { type: 'h2', text: 'Different Layout Approaches by Family Type' },
+      { type: 'p', text: 'A couple has the most flexibility and can dedicate the second bedroom more fully to a study or dressing area, but should resist under-planning storage. A couple with a young child needs the second bedroom to flex without a full redo in a few years, with childproofing built into furniture and electrical point heights. A household needing a permanent work-from-home space should give that zone acoustic and visual separation from high-traffic areas, not just a shared corner of the hall.' },
+      { type: 'callout', text: 'Before finalising your 2 BHK layout, confirm: structural walls identified, primary circulation route mapped, actual furniture clearances measured (not assumed), the second bedroom\'s 3 to 5 year flexibility considered, WFH space electrically planned if needed, and storage treated as a whole-flat system rather than room by room.' },
+    ],
+    faqs: [
+      { q: 'Is it better to have an open kitchen or a closed kitchen in a 2 BHK?', a: 'Both work. Open kitchens make a small flat feel larger but require more daily upkeep discipline since the kitchen is always visible. Closed kitchens contain mess but can make the hall feel more boxed in if not planned with enough of an opening or pass-through.' },
+      { q: 'Should the dining table always be near the kitchen?', a: 'It is the more practical choice for daily use, but not mandatory. Some layouts place dining nearer the main window for better light, accepting a slightly longer serving distance as a trade-off.' },
+      { q: 'Can a 2 BHK genuinely support a full-time work-from-home setup?', a: 'Yes, with deliberate planning that treats the WFH zone as a real design requirement from day one, including electrical points and acoustic separation, rather than retrofitting a desk into whatever space is left over.' },
+    ],
