+  {
+    slug:      'how-to-plan-a-small-living-room',
+    title:     'How to Plan a Small Living Room: Circulation, Scale and Storage First',
+    metaTitle: 'How to Plan a Small Living Room — indeva studio',
+    metaDesc:  'A practical method for planning a small living room: measuring circulation, sizing furniture correctly and zoning, before you choose any decor.',
+    category:  'spatial planning',
+    readTime:  '8 min read',
+    date:      'August 2026',
+    city:      'Delhi NCR',
+    heroImage: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1445116572660-236099ec97a0?w=1200&h=675&fit=crop&q=80',
+    heroAlt:   'small living room furniture layout planning — indeva studio',
+    heroCredit:{ name: 'Nathan Dumlao', url: 'https://unsplash.com/@nate_dumlao' },
+    intro: 'A small living room does not fail because of the wrong colour or the wrong cushions. It fails because furniture was chosen for how it looked in a showroom, not for how it fits the room\'s actual dimensions and the paths people need to walk through it. This is the planning sequence in the order it should actually happen: measure, plan circulation, size furniture, then think about decor.',
+    sections: [
+      { type: 'h2', text: 'Measure the Room Properly First' },
+      { type: 'p', text: 'Get four numbers on paper before anything else: room dimensions at floor level, door swing direction, window positions and sill heights, and any fixed obstructions such as columns or existing electrical points. Draw this to scale, even roughly. Buying furniture against a mental estimate of room size is the single most common cause of a small living room feeling worse after furnishing than before.' },
+      { type: 'h2', text: 'The Small Living Room Planning Formula' },
+      { type: 'list', items: ['Anchor — choose the one wall the seating will be built around, usually the longest unbroken wall', 'Reserve the path — mark the circulation route and treat it as fixed floor area before placing anything else', 'Size the seating to what is left, not the other way around', 'Place the coffee table relative to the seating, roughly 14 to 18 inches from the sofa edge', 'Resolve storage last, fitting it into whatever wall space remains without intruding on circulation'] },
+      { type: 'h2', text: 'How Much Circulation Space to Retain' },
+      { type: 'p', text: 'Working ranges for a small living room: main walking path 30 to 40 inches, space to get up and walk around the coffee table 12 to 18 inches on the open side, and clearance in front of a TV console for someone to pass 18 to 30 inches. Treat the lower end as genuinely minimum — a path used regularly at that width starts to feel tight within weeks.' },
+      { type: 'h2', text: 'Sofa Sizing and When a Sectional Is a Bad Choice' },
+      { type: 'p', text: 'Measure the exact model, not a generic size assumption, and check depth as much as width, since a deep sofa eats circulation space even if it fits the wall. Sectionals are popular but often a poor choice in a genuinely small room: the chaise end typically needs 6 feet of depth, which usually eats an entire circulation path, and a fixed sectional reduces flexibility if the room needs to serve a secondary function like occasional dining or work.' },
+      { type: 'h2', text: 'TV Wall, Coffee Table and Dining Integration' },
+      { type: 'p', text: 'Keep a viewing distance appropriate to screen size, roughly 1.5 to 2.5 times the screen\'s diagonal as a starting point. Size the coffee table to about two-thirds the length of the sofa in front of it — a table the same length or longer tends to crowd the seating zone. If the room also has to absorb dining function, a round or drop-leaf table can recover circulation space when not in use, and keeping the dining zone closer to the kitchen entrance is usually the more practical trade-off.' },
+      { type: 'h2', text: 'Storage, Rugs and Lighting Layers' },
+      { type: 'p', text: 'Prefer storage that goes to the ceiling over storage that stops short, since a shorter unit with unused space above often reads as more cluttered. Use a rug sized so all seating furniture\'s front legs sit on it, at minimum, to reinforce zoning. Layer lighting across ambient, task and accent sources rather than relying on a single central fixture — a room lit only from above almost always reads as smaller and flatter.' },
+      { type: 'h2', text: 'Common Mistakes That Make Small Rooms Feel Smaller' },
+      { type: 'list', items: ['Pushing all furniture flush against the walls, leaving one large awkward empty zone in the middle', 'Choosing furniture with visually heavy legs or skirting that blocks the sightline to the floor', 'Hanging curtains at window-frame width instead of extending the rod beyond the frame', 'Overcrowding one wall with art or shelving while leaving another completely bare'] },
+      { type: 'h2', text: 'Three Example Room Shapes' },
+      { type: 'p', text: 'A narrow rectangular room (roughly 10 by 16 feet) usually works best with seating run along one long wall rather than centred, with a runner rug reinforcing the proportion. A near-square room (roughly 12 by 13 feet) offers the most flexibility but the most temptation to over-furnish — a well-proportioned two-piece seating arrangement with generous circulation usually reads as more spacious than a fully packed three-piece set. An L-shaped living-dining combine should keep the elbow of the L, where circulation paths converge, genuinely clear of furniture, even though it looks like dead space.' },
+    ],
+    faqs: [
+      { q: 'What is the smallest living room size that can comfortably seat 4 to 5 people?', a: 'There is no fixed minimum, since it depends on furniture choice and layout efficiency, but a room around 10 by 12 feet can usually seat 4 to 5 people with careful furniture sizing.' },
+      { q: 'Is a glass coffee table a good choice for a small living room?', a: 'Visually yes, since glass tops read as less heavy and do not block sightlines to the floor. The trade-off is maintenance and the need for tempered, rounded edges, especially with children in the home.' },
+      { q: 'How do I make a small living room feel less cluttered without giving up storage?', a: 'Prioritise closed storage over open shelving unless you will genuinely maintain a curated look, consolidate into fewer taller units rather than several smaller ones, and keep at least one wall visually quiet.' },
+    ],
