+  {
+    slug:      'modular-kitchen-planning-mistakes',
+    title:     'Modular Kitchen Planning Mistakes That Are Cheap to Avoid, Expensive to Fix',
+    metaTitle: 'Modular Kitchen Planning Mistakes — indeva studio',
+    metaDesc:  'The modular kitchen planning mistakes that are cheap to avoid at the design stage and expensive once cabinets are installed, with fixes and timing.',
+    category:  'kitchen design',
+    readTime:  '10 min read',
+    date:      'August 2026',
+    city:      'Delhi NCR',
+    heroImage: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1600210492486-724fe5c67fb0?w=1200&h=675&fit=crop&q=80',
+    heroAlt:   'modular kitchen layout planning mistakes — indeva studio',
+    heroCredit:{ name: 'R ARCHITECTURE', url: 'https://unsplash.com/@rarchitecture_melbourne' },
+    intro: 'Most modular kitchen problems are not visible on installation day. They show up three weeks later, reaching over a hot hob for a drawer, or six months later when the chimney still is not pulling smoke because the ducting was an afterthought. Nearly all of this is cheap to prevent at the planning stage and expensive to fix once cabinets are fabricated.',
+    sections: [
+      { type: 'h2', text: 'Layout Before Aesthetics' },
+      { type: 'p', text: 'A beautiful kitchen with a poor layout is still a poor kitchen to cook in every day. Resolve where the sink, stove, refrigerator and counters sit before choosing a laminate colour or countertop material. The classic work triangle is a useful guideline, not a rigid formula, but the goal is minimising unnecessary crossing and backtracking during a typical wash, prep, cook, plate sequence.' },
+      { type: 'h2', text: 'Counter Height and Counter Space' },
+      { type: 'p', text: 'Counter height is one of the most personal decisions in kitchen planning and is too often defaulted to a generic number without checking the primary cook\'s height and posture. Separately, plan for landing space, not just the hob and sink footprint — at least 18 to 24 inches of clear counter on one side of both is a practical minimum, since less turns basic prep into a juggling act.' },
+      { type: 'h2', text: 'Appliances and Electrical Points' },
+      { type: 'p', text: 'Confirm exact appliance models and their cutout dimensions before cabinet drawings are finalised — even an inch of difference between brands can mean a cabinet does not fit. Electrical points for the chimney, hob, oven, water purifier and countertop appliances need to be marked against confirmed appliance positions before the false ceiling closes and cabinets go in, since retrofitting later usually means visible conduit.' },
+      { type: 'h2', text: 'Ventilation, Storage and Corner Solutions' },
+      { type: 'p', text: 'A chimney is only as effective as its ducting — long runs and unnecessary bends reduce suction regardless of unit power, and this needs planning before the false ceiling closes off access. Storage volume should reflect actual household cooking and buying habits, not a generic allocation, and drawers generally suit daily-use items better than shelves behind a door. Corner cabinets need a proper carousel or pull-out mechanism, or a meaningful share of that space becomes effectively unusable.' },
+      { type: 'h2', text: 'Sink, Refrigerator and Material Choices' },
+      { type: 'p', text: 'Keep the sink close to the existing plumbing stack — moving it far is one of the more expensive layout changes once civil work is complete. Refrigerators need clearance for door swing and ventilation on at least one side, per the specific model\'s requirements. Material choices should be matched to realistic household maintenance habits — porous stone without sealing stains easily near typical Indian cooking, while high-gloss shutters show fingerprints more readily than matte finishes.' },
+      { type: 'h2', text: 'Lighting and Trade Coordination' },
+      { type: 'p', text: 'General ceiling lighting alone leaves the counter, where the actual work happens, in the cook\'s own shadow — under-cabinet task lighting is a frequently skipped detail with an outsized daily impact. Most of the mistakes above compound from one root cause: modular installation happening before civil work, plumbing and electrical points are complete and coordinated against the final layout drawing. Sequencing this correctly, with a single coordinated drawing reviewed by every trade, avoids on-site improvisation.' },
+      { type: 'callout', text: 'Before ordering cabinets: layout and work sequence finalised, counter height set against the primary cook, all appliance models confirmed, electrical points marked against confirmed appliance positions, chimney ducting route planned, storage volume matched to real household habits, corner mechanisms selected, sink position checked against the plumbing stack, and installation sequenced after civil, plumbing and electrical work is complete.' },
+    ],
+    faqs: [
+      { q: 'Is it possible to fix a poorly planned modular kitchen after installation?', a: 'Some issues like lighting or minor storage reorganisation are fixable with moderate effort. Sink position relative to plumbing, counter height and appliance clearances usually require significant, expensive rework.' },
+      { q: 'How early should electrical points be finalised in a modular kitchen project?', a: 'Before the false ceiling and tiling are executed, and ideally before the final cabinet drawing is signed off, since points need to align with confirmed appliance positions.' },
+      { q: 'Do I need a chimney if I have good natural ventilation?', a: 'It depends on cooking style and actual airflow, but for most enclosed apartment kitchens with regular Indian-style cooking, a chimney meaningfully improves air quality and reduces residue over time.' },
+    ],
