+    heroImage: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1600210492486-724fe5c67fb0?w=1200&h=675&fit=crop&q=80',
+    heroAlt:   'wardrobe internal layout and depth planning — indeva studio',
+    heroCredit:{ name: 'R ARCHITECTURE', url: 'https://unsplash.com/@rarchitecture_melbourne' },
+    intro: 'A wardrobe gets chosen the same way most furniture does, by shutter finish and handle style. But the decision that determines whether it works for years is almost entirely internal: depth, hanging clearance, shelf allocation, and how space is divided between hanging, folding and drawer storage. This is about that internal logic, the part that does not show up in a rendered elevation but shows up every morning.',
+    sections: [
+      { type: 'h2', text: 'Standard Depth Is a Starting Point, Not a Rule' },
+      { type: 'p', text: 'There is a commonly referenced depth range for comfortable hanger clearance, but the real number depends on shutter type, hardware, and hanger depth itself. Hinged shutters generally allow the full internal depth to be used efficiently since the door swings clear. Sliding shutters need internal clearance for the track, which marginally reduces usable depth compared to the external footprint.' },
+      { type: 'h2', text: 'Long-Hang vs. Short-Hang Storage' },
+      { type: 'p', text: 'Long-hang, for full-length garments, needs close to the full height of its section. Short-hang needs roughly half that vertical space, meaning a shelf or drawer bank can be stacked below it, using height more efficiently than long-hang storage would in the same space. Getting this ratio wrong against actual household needs results in either wasted vertical space or a wardrobe that runs out of hanging room while shelves sit half-empty.' },
+      { type: 'h2', text: 'Sliding vs. Hinged Wardrobes' },
+      { type: 'p', text: 'Sliding wardrobes need no floor clearance to open but only expose half the width at a time, and suit rooms where floor space in front of the wardrobe is limited. Hinged wardrobes need door-swing clearance but give full-width access when open, and suit rooms with adequate space. Neither is universally better — the choice depends on room layout and clearance.' },
+      { type: 'h2', text: 'Shelves, Drawers and Loft' },
+      { type: 'p', text: 'Shelves do not need to match the wardrobe\'s full depth to be useful — excessively deep shelves often become inefficient as items get pushed to the back and forgotten. Drawers suit smaller, frequently accessed items better than open shelves, since contents stay contained rather than stacked and disorganised. Loft space above a wardrobe suits infrequently accessed items like luggage and off-season clothing, and should be sized against what is actually intended to be stored there, not treated as generic extra space.' },
+      { type: 'h2', text: 'Planning for Children and Couples' },
+      { type: 'p', text: 'Children\'s wardrobes benefit from lower, reachable rods and shelves for everyday items, plus adjustable fittings that accommodate growth without a full redesign every couple of years. A shared wardrobe for a couple needs an honest allocation conversation, not an assumed even split — actual clothing volume, hanging versus folding preference, and whether both people need simultaneous morning access all affect what layout will genuinely work.' },
+      { type: 'h2', text: 'Corner Wardrobes and Handleless Designs' },
+      { type: 'p', text: 'Corner wardrobes can use an awkward room corner efficiently, but the innermost section is harder to access and is usually better allocated to infrequently used storage than daily items. Handleless designs using push-to-open mechanisms give a cleaner visual line but can require more deliberate effort to open, and profile handles need positioning that will not catch on clothing — both worth testing in person before committing.' },
+      { type: 'callout', text: 'Before finalising a wardrobe: confirm available room depth against shutter type, decide the realistic long-hang to short-hang split for the household, separate daily-access storage from infrequently used storage, plan shoe and luggage storage as part of the same decision, and for shared wardrobes, agree allocation explicitly rather than assuming an even split.' },
+    ],
+    faqs: [
+      { q: 'Is a deeper wardrobe always better?', a: 'No. Beyond the depth needed for comfortable hanger clearance, additional depth mostly benefits storage volume at the cost of items being pushed to the back and forgotten. Well-organised internal zoning usually matters more than maximising depth.' },
+      { q: 'Should I choose sliding or hinged shutters for a small bedroom?', a: 'If floor space in front of the wardrobe is limited, sliding shutters avoid needing door-swing clearance. If space allows and full-width access is a priority, hinged shutters are worth considering instead.' },
+      { q: 'Is loft storage worth the extra cost?', a: 'For most households yes, since it gains storage volume for infrequently used items without consuming additional floor area, provided it is sized appropriately for what will actually be stored there.' },
+    ],
+  },
+
+  {
+    slug:      'kitchen-counter-height-india',
+    title:     'Kitchen Counter Height in India: How to Decide the Right Number for Your Household',
+    metaTitle: 'Kitchen Counter Height in India — indeva studio',
+    metaDesc:  'Kitchen counter height is not one universal number. Here is how the primary cook\'s height and working posture should actually decide it.',
+    category:  'kitchen design',
+    readTime:  '7 min read',
+    date:      'August 2026',
+    city:      'Delhi NCR',
+    heroImage: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1445116572660-236099ec97a0?w=1200&h=675&fit=crop&q=80',
+    heroAlt:   'kitchen counter height ergonomics in India — indeva studio',
+    heroCredit:{ name: 'Nathan Dumlao', url: 'https://unsplash.com/@nate_dumlao' },
+    intro: 'Ask what the standard kitchen counter height in India is and most sources give a single number, as if every counter in the country should be built to the same height regardless of who cooks at it. Counter height should be an ergonomic decision based primarily on the height and working posture of whoever cooks most often in that kitchen, not a generic average.',
+    sections: [
+      { type: 'h2', text: 'Why One Standard Height Does Not Work for Everyone' },
+      { type: 'p', text: 'Most modular kitchens in India are built within a broad common range, and contractors often default to a mid-range figure unless specifically asked to customise. This works reasonably well for an average-height user but is a compromise, not a personalised fit. A counter built for an average height is too high for a shorter cook, causing raised shoulders and arm fatigue, and too low for a taller cook, causing back strain — and since a counter is used daily for years, even a small mismatch compounds into real physical strain.' },
+      { type: 'h2', text: 'How User Height Should Drive the Decision' },
+      { type: 'p', text: 'The general ergonomic reasoning is to plan work-surface height relative to the cook\'s elbow height when standing naturally, minus a small allowance for comfortable working clearance, which keeps wrists and forearms in a neutral position during repetitive tasks like chopping. This is a principle, not a rigid formula — the final height also depends on countertop thickness, footwear habits, and the specific tasks the counter is used for most.' },
+      { type: 'h2', text: 'Finished Floor Level, Countertop Thickness and Plinth' },
+      { type: 'p', text: 'Counter height should always be calculated from the finished floor level after tiling, not the bare floor during construction. Total counter height is the sum of plinth height, base cabinet height, and countertop thickness, which varies by material — confirm the exact thickness of your chosen countertop before finalising base cabinet height, since the two need to add up to the target total.' },
+      { type: 'h2', text: 'Multi-User Kitchens and Island Height' },
+      { type: 'p', text: 'When multiple people of different heights regularly cook in the same kitchen, there is no single height that suits everyone, and this is a genuine trade-off rather than a problem with a perfect solution. Reasonable approaches include building around whoever cooks most frequently, using a stepped counter with two heights where layout allows, or a step stool as a low-cost partial fix. Island and breakfast counters are often built taller to suit bar-stool seating, which is a separate decision from the main prep counter\'s ergonomic height and should not be conflated with it.' },
+      { type: 'h2', text: 'Common Mistakes in Counter Sizing' },
+      { type: 'list', items: ['Defaulting to a standard height without ever discussing the primary cook\'s actual height or posture concerns', 'Finalising height before the exact countertop material and thickness is chosen', 'Calculating from the bare, unfinished floor rather than the finished floor level', 'Ignoring a genuinely multi-user kitchen and building solely around one person\'s height', 'Assuming island and prep counter height should automatically match when they serve different functions'] },
+      { type: 'h2', text: 'How to Decide Before Construction' },
+      { type: 'p', text: 'Identify the primary cook, estimate elbow height standing naturally in typical kitchen footwear, confirm countertop material and thickness before finalising base cabinet height, calculate from finished floor level, decide island height separately based on seating function, and document the final number clearly in the design drawing before cabinet fabrication begins.' },
+    ],
+    faqs: [
+      { q: 'What is the standard kitchen counter height in India?', a: 'There is a commonly used default range most contractors build to unless asked otherwise, but it is a reasonable starting reference, not a number that should be assumed correct without checking against the primary cook.' },
+      { q: 'Is a taller counter better for avoiding back pain?', a: 'Not universally. A counter too tall relative to the user causes its own strain through raised shoulders and arm fatigue, just as one too short causes back strain from stooping. The goal is matching height to the user.' },
+      { q: 'Should I customise my kitchen counter height, or is it too complicated?', a: 'It is a straightforward adjustment at the planning stage, primarily affecting plinth height, base cabinet height, or countertop selection, all of which are decided before fabrication anyway.' },
+    ],
+  },
+
