+<header class="article-header">
+  <span class="article-category">technical planning</span>
+  <h1>How to Plan Electrical Points Before Interior Work Begins</h1>
+  <div class="article-meta">
+    <span>August 2026</span><span class="dot">·</span>
+    <span>9 min read</span><span class="dot">·</span>
+    <span>indéva studio</span>
+    <span class="dot">·</span><span>Delhi NCR</span>
+  </div>
+</header>
+
+<div class="hero">
+  <img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1445116572660-236099ec97a0?w=1200&amp;h=675&amp;fit=crop&amp;q=80" alt="electrical point planning for home interiors — indeva studio" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high"/>
+  <p class="hero-credit">photo by <a href="https://unsplash.com/@nate_dumlao?utm_source=indeva_studio&utm_medium=referral" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Nathan Dumlao</a> on <a href="https://unsplash.com/?utm_source=indeva_studio&utm_medium=referral" target="_blank" rel="noopener">unsplash</a></p>
+</div>
+
+<article class="article-body">
+  <p class="intro">Electrical planning is the part of interior work most likely to be treated as an afterthought, and the part most expensive to fix once it is wrong. Once conduit is chased into the wall and the false ceiling closes over the slab, moving a switch or adding a socket means breaking into finished work. This explains why planning has to come before furniture and false ceiling execution, not after.</p>
+  <div class="callout"><p>This article explains planning logic, not a DIY wiring guide. Final load calculations, wire sizing, circuit protection, earthing and installation must be carried out by a licensed electrician in line with applicable local safety requirements.</p></div>
+  <h2>Why Sequencing Matters</h2>
+  <p>The order should be furniture layout, then electrical point marking, then wiring, then false ceiling, then final finishes. Reversing this is the most common cause of a socket ending up hidden behind a wardrobe, a switch positioned where a door swing makes it awkward, or a missing point exactly where a lamp or charger was always going to sit.</p>
+  <h2>Living Room and Bedrooms</h2>
+  <p>In the living room, plan points behind the TV console position, near the router location, and at seating points for lamps rather than relying on overhead lighting alone. Consider two-way switching for rooms with two main entrances. In bedrooms, bedside points on both sides of the bed are frequently underestimated, especially for a shared bed where both occupants need independent charging and lamp access. AC point rating and position depend on whether the unit is split or ductable, and this should be confirmed before finalising.</p>
+  <h2>Kitchen and Bathrooms</h2>
+  <p>Kitchen points need to account for countertop appliances, a correctly rated point for the chimney and hob, the refrigerator, the water purifier, and the washing machine if it is located in or near the kitchen. Bathroom electrical planning carries additional safety considerations given the presence of water, and appropriate point placement and protection for wet areas should always be confirmed directly with your electrician rather than assumed.</p>
+  <h2>Study Points, Charging and AC Planning</h2>
+  <p>Any dedicated work-from-home space needs its own power point, task lighting, and if needed a data point for a more stable connection than Wi-Fi alone. Plan for more charging points than feels strictly necessary near beds, desks and seating areas, since device needs tend to grow over a home&#39;s lifetime. AC point requirements differ between split and ductable systems and should be settled at the same stage as false ceiling planning, since ductable units affect ceiling design directly.</p>
+  <h2>Switchboard Placement and Backup Planning</h2>
+  <p>Entry switches should sit on the side of the door that opens away from the switch, and switch height should suit the household&#39;s actual users. If a backup power source is planned, decide early which circuits need coverage — typically essential lighting and the refrigerator, rather than the entire home&#39;s load, since this affects how circuits are wired from the start.</p>
+  <h2>Coordination Across Trades</h2>
+  <p>Electrical planning has to be cross-checked against the false ceiling design for recessed lighting and AC ducting clearance, the kitchen layout for appliance points, and the furniture layout for every other room. A single coordinated working drawing, reviewed jointly by the interior designer and the electrician, is worth the time investment it takes upfront.</p>
+  <div class="ornament"><div class="ornament-line"></div><span class="ornament-gem">◆</span><div class="ornament-line"></div></div>
+</article>
+
+
+<section class="faq-section">
+  <h2>frequently asked questions</h2>
+  <details><summary>Can electrical points be added after the false ceiling and walls are finished?</summary><p>Technically yes, but it usually means visible surface conduit or breaking into finished plaster or ceiling. Planning ahead avoids both.</p></details><details><summary>How many electrical points does a typical room need?</summary><p>There is no single correct number — it depends entirely on the furniture layout and the household&#39;s actual appliance and device use, which is why furniture-first planning matters more than a generic point count.</p></details><details><summary>Who should be involved in finalising the electrical plan?</summary><p>Both the interior designer and the electrician, ideally together. One understands the space planning intent, the other understands load calculations and safety requirements.</p></details>
+</section>
