+  <span class="article-category">technical planning</span>
+  <h1>False Ceiling Height: How to Decide What Actually Works for Your Room</h1>
+  <div class="article-meta">
+    <span>August 2026</span><span class="dot">·</span>
+    <span>8 min read</span><span class="dot">·</span>
+    <span>indéva studio</span>
+    <span class="dot">·</span><span>Delhi NCR</span>
+  </div>
+</header>
+
+<div class="hero">
+  <img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1600210492486-724fe5c67fb0?w=1200&amp;h=675&amp;fit=crop&amp;q=80" alt="false ceiling height planning diagram — indeva studio" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high"/>
+  <p class="hero-credit">photo by <a href="https://unsplash.com/@rarchitecture_melbourne?utm_source=indeva_studio&utm_medium=referral" target="_blank" rel="noopener">R ARCHITECTURE</a> on <a href="https://unsplash.com/?utm_source=indeva_studio&utm_medium=referral" target="_blank" rel="noopener">unsplash</a></p>
+</div>
+
+<article class="article-body">
+  <p class="intro">Search for false ceiling height and most pages confidently state a single number, as if every room in every building should drop to exactly the same height. The right false ceiling height is the result of several specific measurements: your actual slab-to-floor height, what services need concealing, which ceiling system you use, and what proportions feel comfortable in that specific room.</p>
+  <h2>What Determines Whether a False Ceiling Is Suitable at All</h2>
+  <p>A false ceiling makes sense when there are services to conceal, such as AC ducting, exposed conduit or an uneven ceiling, or when layered and cove lighting are wanted. It is less justified when slab height is already limited, nothing needs concealing, and the motivation is purely aesthetic — the height lost may not be worth the visual gain in a room already on the shorter side.</p>
+  <h2>Measure Slab-to-Floor Height First</h2>
+  <p>Measure from the underside of the structural slab, or the lowest beam, to the current floor surface, not an assumed number. This varies meaningfully by building age, developer specification and city, and it is the single most important number in the entire decision, since everything else is subtracted from it. If new flooring is being laid, its thickness including the bedding layer reduces usable height further and should be included in the calculation.</p>
+  <h2>Minimum Practical Clearance vs. Visually Comfortable Proportion</h2>
+  <p>These are two different thresholds. Minimum practical clearance is the lowest height at which the room stays functionally comfortable, and it depends on floor area and the occupants&#39; own height, not a fixed universal number. Visually comfortable proportion is a separate, more subjective threshold — a room can clear the functional minimum and still feel visually squat if the drop is large relative to its floor area. Evaluate your specific room against both, rather than a generic recommendation.</p>
+  <h2>How Much Height Different Systems Consume</h2>
+  <p>A simple flat gypsum board with only wiring to conceal needs relatively shallow depth. Cove or shadow-gap detailing adds depth beyond the flat plane. A ceiling concealing an AC ductable unit needs substantially more, and the ducting itself, not the board, usually drives the requirement — this must be confirmed with your AC vendor or MEP consultant before finalising the drop, since a cavity too shallow for the ducting is a difficult mistake to fix later.</p>
+  <h2>Lighting, Curtain Pockets and Beam Concealment</h2>
+  <p>Recessed downlights need a minimum housing depth that should be checked against your planned drop; surface-mounted fixtures avoid this constraint entirely. Cove lighting needs its own dedicated depth and width allowance designed in from the start. Curtain pockets need enough depth for the specific track and heading style, or the track stays visible. If a structural beam runs through the room, boxing it separately while keeping the rest of the ceiling higher usually preserves more height than dropping the entire ceiling to clear it uniformly.</p>
+  <h2>A Decision Framework</h2>
+  <ul><li>Do you have services genuinely requiring concealment? If yes, at least a partial false ceiling is likely justified</li><li>What is your actual measured slab-to-floor height, not an assumed one</li><li>What is the minimum system depth required, confirmed with your AC vendor and electrician</li><li>Does the remaining height, after flooring and system depth, clear both the functional minimum and a proportion you are comfortable with for this specific room</li><li>If not, consider a partial false ceiling — a perimeter cove or a drop only over the zone requiring concealment — rather than a full-room drop</li></ul>
+  <div class="callout"><p>Do not copy a ceiling design from a photo without first confirming your own slab height, services and proportions support it. A ceiling that looks striking in an image was designed for that specific room&#39;s constraints, none of which are visible in the photo.</p></div>
+  <div class="ornament"><div class="ornament-line"></div><span class="ornament-gem">◆</span><div class="ornament-line"></div></div>
+</article>
+
+
+<section class="faq-section">
+  <h2>frequently asked questions</h2>
+  <details><summary>Is there a minimum false ceiling height required by building code in India?</summary><p>This depends on local municipal bylaws and jurisdiction, which vary. If this is a genuine concern, particularly for commercial spaces, confirm applicable requirements with a licensed architect or your local municipal authority rather than relying on a generic number.</p></details><details><summary>Can I install a false ceiling in a room with a low slab height?</summary><p>Often yes, but the design needs to adapt — a shallow-cavity system with minimal cove detailing and surface-mounted rather than deep-housing recessed lights can work even where height is limited.</p></details><details><summary>Do I need a false ceiling if I am not installing an AC or complex lighting?</summary><p>Not necessarily. If the existing ceiling is level, undamaged, and there is no wiring or conduit to conceal, a fresh coat of paint may achieve a clean look without sacrificing height.</p></details>
+</section>
