+<header class="article-header">
+  <span class="article-category">furniture &amp; storage</span>
+  <h1>Wardrobe Depth and Internal Layout: Why the Inside Matters More Than the Outside</h1>
+  <div class="article-meta">
+    <span>August 2026</span><span class="dot">·</span>
+    <span>8 min read</span><span class="dot">·</span>
+    <span>indéva studio</span>
+    <span class="dot">·</span><span>Delhi NCR</span>
+  </div>
+</header>
+
+<div class="hero">
+  <img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1600210492486-724fe5c67fb0?w=1200&amp;h=675&amp;fit=crop&amp;q=80" alt="wardrobe internal layout and depth planning — indeva studio" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high"/>
+  <p class="hero-credit">photo by <a href="https://unsplash.com/@rarchitecture_melbourne?utm_source=indeva_studio&utm_medium=referral" target="_blank" rel="noopener">R ARCHITECTURE</a> on <a href="https://unsplash.com/?utm_source=indeva_studio&utm_medium=referral" target="_blank" rel="noopener">unsplash</a></p>
+</div>
+
+<article class="article-body">
+  <p class="intro">A wardrobe gets chosen the same way most furniture does, by shutter finish and handle style. But the decision that determines whether it works for years is almost entirely internal: depth, hanging clearance, shelf allocation, and how space is divided between hanging, folding and drawer storage. This is about that internal logic, the part that does not show up in a rendered elevation but shows up every morning.</p>
+  <h2>Standard Depth Is a Starting Point, Not a Rule</h2>
+  <p>There is a commonly referenced depth range for comfortable hanger clearance, but the real number depends on shutter type, hardware, and hanger depth itself. Hinged shutters generally allow the full internal depth to be used efficiently since the door swings clear. Sliding shutters need internal clearance for the track, which marginally reduces usable depth compared to the external footprint.</p>
+  <h2>Long-Hang vs. Short-Hang Storage</h2>
+  <p>Long-hang, for full-length garments, needs close to the full height of its section. Short-hang needs roughly half that vertical space, meaning a shelf or drawer bank can be stacked below it, using height more efficiently than long-hang storage would in the same space. Getting this ratio wrong against actual household needs results in either wasted vertical space or a wardrobe that runs out of hanging room while shelves sit half-empty.</p>
+  <h2>Sliding vs. Hinged Wardrobes</h2>
+  <p>Sliding wardrobes need no floor clearance to open but only expose half the width at a time, and suit rooms where floor space in front of the wardrobe is limited. Hinged wardrobes need door-swing clearance but give full-width access when open, and suit rooms with adequate space. Neither is universally better — the choice depends on room layout and clearance.</p>
+  <h2>Shelves, Drawers and Loft</h2>
+  <p>Shelves do not need to match the wardrobe&#39;s full depth to be useful — excessively deep shelves often become inefficient as items get pushed to the back and forgotten. Drawers suit smaller, frequently accessed items better than open shelves, since contents stay contained rather than stacked and disorganised. Loft space above a wardrobe suits infrequently accessed items like luggage and off-season clothing, and should be sized against what is actually intended to be stored there, not treated as generic extra space.</p>
+  <h2>Planning for Children and Couples</h2>
+  <p>Children&#39;s wardrobes benefit from lower, reachable rods and shelves for everyday items, plus adjustable fittings that accommodate growth without a full redesign every couple of years. A shared wardrobe for a couple needs an honest allocation conversation, not an assumed even split — actual clothing volume, hanging versus folding preference, and whether both people need simultaneous morning access all affect what layout will genuinely work.</p>
+  <h2>Corner Wardrobes and Handleless Designs</h2>
+  <p>Corner wardrobes can use an awkward room corner efficiently, but the innermost section is harder to access and is usually better allocated to infrequently used storage than daily items. Handleless designs using push-to-open mechanisms give a cleaner visual line but can require more deliberate effort to open, and profile handles need positioning that will not catch on clothing — both worth testing in person before committing.</p>
+  <div class="callout"><p>Before finalising a wardrobe: confirm available room depth against shutter type, decide the realistic long-hang to short-hang split for the household, separate daily-access storage from infrequently used storage, plan shoe and luggage storage as part of the same decision, and for shared wardrobes, agree allocation explicitly rather than assuming an even split.</p></div>
+  <div class="ornament"><div class="ornament-line"></div><span class="ornament-gem">◆</span><div class="ornament-line"></div></div>
+</article>
+
+
+<section class="faq-section">
+  <h2>frequently asked questions</h2>
+  <details><summary>Is a deeper wardrobe always better?</summary><p>No. Beyond the depth needed for comfortable hanger clearance, additional depth mostly benefits storage volume at the cost of items being pushed to the back and forgotten. Well-organised internal zoning usually matters more than maximising depth.</p></details><details><summary>Should I choose sliding or hinged shutters for a small bedroom?</summary><p>If floor space in front of the wardrobe is limited, sliding shutters avoid needing door-swing clearance. If space allows and full-width access is a priority, hinged shutters are worth considering instead.</p></details><details><summary>Is loft storage worth the extra cost?</summary><p>For most households yes, since it gains storage volume for infrequently used items without consuming additional floor area, provided it is sized appropriately for what will actually be stored there.</p></details>
+</section>
